>> title: Som Tum
>> category: Salads
>> tags: papaya, thai, salad, chiles, no-cook
>> counters: Thai Kitchen
>> aka: som tam, som tum thai, som tam thai, green papaya salad, papaya salad, tam mak hoong
>> servings: 2
>> time: 20 min
>> step.1: pound to a rough paste
>> step.2: bruise in
>> step.3: stir in
>> step.4: bruise in 2 min
>> step.5: turn out

Pound @garlic{2%cloves}, @Thai chiles{3}(stemmed) and @kosher salt{1/4%tsp} to a rough paste in a tall clay #mortar{}. Three chiles is a tourist's plate and ten is a Thai one; this is the number the woman at the mortar changes when you tell her how you want it.

Bruise @long beans{3}(cut in 1-in lengths) and @cherry tomatoes{6}(halved) into @&(~1)paste{} — a few strokes each, cracking them rather than crushing them.

Stir @palm sugar{1%Tbs}(shaved), @fish sauce{2%Tbs}, @lime juice{2%Tbs} and @dried shrimp{1%Tbs} into @&(~1)mortar{} until the sugar dissolves. This is som tum Thai, the peanut-and-dried-shrimp version; the Lao one swaps the shrimp for fermented fish and is much funkier.

Bruise @green papaya{3%cups}(shredded long and thin; about half a small unripe one) into @&(~1)dressing{} for ~toss{2%min}, one hand pounding and the other turning it over with a spoon, until every strand is wet but the papaya still snaps.

Turn @&(~1)salad{} out onto a plate with @roasted peanuts{2%Tbs}(coarsely crushed). It is made one plate at a time, in front of you, and it does not keep — a batch of it in a bowl is a different, sadder dish an hour later.
